{"test_id": "B22-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Can a CII operator apply for a waiver for MFA requirements on legacy SCADA systems?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Waiver application must include strong compensating controls", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Network isolation and monitoring can provide equivalent protection", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Waiver is temporary and must include transition planning", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Legacy SCADA cannot support MFA**\n\n**Sector Context:** Energy CII\n\n**Decision:** Yes, with compensating controls\n\n**Reasoning:**\nOT systems cannot always support modern authentication. Compensating controls required: network isolation, monitoring, strong authentication at network boundary.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Waiver application must include strong compensating controls (critical priority)\n- Network isolation and monitoring can provide equivalent protection (critical priority)\n- Waiver is temporary and must include transition planning (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Legacy SCADA cannot support MFA", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Is cost a valid justification for a waiver from log retention requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Cost constraints do not exempt CCoP compliance", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Log compression and tiered storage can manage costs effectively", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Waiver may be considered only if requirement is technically infeasible", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: 12-month log retention storage cost**\n\n**Sector Context:** Healthcare CII\n\n**Decision:** No, waiver not appropriate\n\n**Reasoning:**\nCost alone is not sufficient grounds for waiver under Section 11(7). Organizational measures must be taken to meet requirements, including log compression and tiered storage.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Cost constraints do not exempt CCoP compliance (critical priority)\n- Log compression and tiered storage can manage costs effectively (important priority)\n- Waiver may be considered only if requirement is technically infeasible (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: 12-month log retention storage cost", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Can limited staff justify a waiver from quarterly access review requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Resource constraints do not exempt compliance obligations", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Automation can reduce manual review burden while maintaining control", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Modified approach still requires CSA approval", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Small team cannot manage quarterly access reviews**\n\n**Sector Context:** Banking CII\n\n**Decision:** No, but modified approach may work\n\n**Reasoning:**\nStaffing constraints are not grounds for waiver. However, automation can reduce manual effort. Semi-annual reviews with automated monitoring may be acceptable if risk-assessed.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Resource constraints do not exempt compliance obligations (critical priority)\n- Automation can reduce manual review burden while maintaining control (important priority)\n- Modified approach still requires CSA approval (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Small team cannot manage quarterly access reviews", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What is the waiver process for OT systems that cannot be patched during active operations?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Safety-critical operations may justify delayed patching", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Compensating controls must be documented and implemented", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Planned maintenance windows must be used for patching", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: OT system cannot be patched during operations**\n\n**Sector Context:** Government CII\n\n**Decision:** Yes, with time limit\n\n**Reasoning:**\nOT patching during operations creates safety risk. Time-limited waiver for patching during scheduled maintenance windows. Compensating controls: strict network isolation, monitoring, allow-listing.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Safety-critical operations may justify delayed patching (critical priority)\n- Compensating controls must be documented and implemented (critical priority)\n- Planned maintenance windows must be used for patching (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: OT system cannot be patched during operations", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Can lack of in-house CISO expertise justify outsourcing the CISO role?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CISO role can be outsourced but accountability cannot be delegated", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Third-party CISO must have appropriate qualifications and experience", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Service provider must meet CCoP compliance requirements themselves", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: No local CISO expertise available**\n\n**Sector Context:** Telecommunications CII\n\n**Decision:** Yes, role can be outsourced\n\n**Reasoning:**\nCISO role can be fulfilled by qualified third-party or shared service. However, accountability remains with CIIO. Service level agreements must meet CCoP requirements.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- CISO role can be outsourced but accountability cannot be delegated (critical priority)\n- Third-party CISO must have appropriate qualifications and experience (critical priority)\n- Service provider must meet CCoP compliance requirements themselves (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: No local CISO expertise available", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "How should a waiver request be structured when encryption breaks legacy systems?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Technical incompatibility must be documented with evidence", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Compensating controls must provide equivalent protection", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Transition plan must have specific timeline and milestones", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Encryption incompatible with legacy system**\n\n**Sector Context:** Water CII\n\n**Decision:** Yes, with transition plan\n\n**Reasoning:**\nEncryption incompatibility may justify waiver with transition plan. Must demonstrate: incompatibility evidence, compensating controls (network isolation, data diode), transition roadmap to encryption-capable system.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Technical incompatibility must be documented with evidence (critical priority)\n- Compensating controls must provide equivalent protection (critical priority)\n- Transition plan must have specific timeline and milestones (critical priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Encryption incompatible with legacy system", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What happens when CCoP requirements conflict with another regulator's requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP obligations prevail in regulatory conflicts under Section 11", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Must document the conflict and seek CSA guidance formally", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Other regulator may be consulted during resolution process", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Conflicting regulatory requirements**\n\n**Sector Context:** Transportation CII\n\n**Decision:** Consult CSA for guidance\n\n**Reasoning:**\nRegulatory conflicts require CSA consultation. CCoP 2.0 Section 11 states CII obligations prevail over conflicting requirements. Document conflict and seek CSA guidance on appropriate approach.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- CCoP obligations prevail in regulatory conflicts under Section 11 (critical priority)\n- Must document the conflict and seek CSA guidance formally (critical priority)\n- Other regulator may be consulted during resolution process (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Conflicting regulatory requirements", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Can a waiver apply to only part of a system rather than the entire CII system?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Partial waiver must have clearly defined system boundaries", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Rest of system must achieve full CCoP compliance", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Boundary controls must protect compliant portions from non-compliant subsystem", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Partial waiver for subsystem**\n\n**Sector Context:** Energy CII\n\n**Decision:** Yes, partial waiver possible\n\n**Reasoning:**\nPartial waivers may be granted for specific subsystems that cannot comply while rest of system complies. Must clearly define scope and implement controls for compliant portions. Boundary protection required.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Partial waiver must have clearly defined system boundaries (critical priority)\n- Rest of system must achieve full CCoP compliance (critical priority)\n- Boundary controls must protect compliant portions from non-compliant subsystem (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Partial waiver for subsystem", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What options exist when vendor contract prevents CCoP-required controls?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Vendor non-compliance does not exempt operator responsibility", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Contract termination may be necessary for critical controls", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Risk acceptance requires formal documentation and CSA notification", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Vendor refuses to implement security controls**\n\n**Sector Context:** Healthcare CII\n\n**Decision:** Risk acceptance or contract termination\n\n**Reasoning:**\nIf vendor absolutely cannot implement required controls, options include: 1) terminate contract and find new vendor, 2) implement compensating controls at operator's boundary, 3) formal risk acceptance if no alternative exists.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Vendor non-compliance does not exempt operator responsibility (critical priority)\n- Contract termination may be necessary for critical controls (important priority)\n- Risk acceptance requires formal documentation and CSA notification (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Vendor refuses to implement security controls", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What is required for waiver renewal and how does it differ from initial application?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Waiver renewal requires showing continued infeasibility", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Compensating control effectiveness must be demonstrated with evidence", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "CSA may tighten requirements or reduce waiver scope on renewal", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Waiver renewal approaching**\n\n**Sector Context:** Banking CII\n\n**Decision:** Waiver renewal requires continued justification\n\n**Reasoning:**\nWaiver renewal requires: demonstration of continued infeasibility, effectiveness of compensating controls, progress on transition plan, alternative approaches considered. CSA may reduce scope or deny if circumstances changed.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Waiver renewal requires showing continued infeasibility (critical priority)\n- Compensating control effectiveness must be demonstrated with evidence (critical priority)\n- CSA may tighten requirements or reduce waiver scope on renewal (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Waiver renewal approaching", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Does bypassing security controls during emergency constitute a waiver?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Emergency bypass is procedural, not a regulatory waiver", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Documented procedures must include authorization and restoration", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Incident report should document any emergency bypass usage", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Emergency bypass of security controls**\n\n**Sector Context:** Government CII\n\n**Decision:** Not a waiver, but must be documented\n\n**Reasoning:**\nEmergency control bypass is not a waiver but must be documented. Emergency procedures should define when bypass is permitted, who authorizes, what compensating measures apply, and how normal controls are restored.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Emergency bypass is procedural, not a regulatory waiver (critical priority)\n- Documented procedures must include authorization and restoration (critical priority)\n- Incident report should document any emergency bypass usage (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Emergency bypass of security controls", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Should separate waivers be filed for each non-compliant area or one comprehensive waiver?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Separate waivers allow targeted compensating controls for each gap", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Common root cause may justify comprehensive waiver approach", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "CSA guidance should be sought on preferred approach", "tier": "supporting", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Multiple non-compliant areas**\n\n**Sector Context:** Telecommunications CII\n\n**Decision:** Case-by-case determination\n\n**Reasoning:**\nSeparate waivers for each area with distinct justifications are preferred. However, if non-compliance stems from single root cause (e.g., legacy platform), comprehensive waiver may be more appropriate. CSA may prefer specific waivers.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Separate waivers allow targeted compensating controls for each gap (important priority)\n- Common root cause may justify comprehensive waiver approach (important priority)\n- CSA guidance should be sought on preferred approach (supporting priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Multiple non-compliant areas", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What waiver approach works for legacy OT protocols with no security features?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Legacy OT protocols have inherent security limitations", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Network isolation is primary compensating control for OT", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Protocol gateways enable monitoring at boundary", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: OT protocol cannot be secured**\n\n**Sector Context:** Water CII\n\n**Decision:** Yes, with strong compensating controls\n\n**Reasoning:**\nLegacy OT protocols (Modbus, DNP3 without security) cannot be patched. Waiver required with strong compensating: network isolation, protocol gateways, deep packet inspection, anomaly detection. Long-term replacement planning.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Legacy OT protocols have inherent security limitations (critical priority)\n- Network isolation is primary compensating control for OT (critical priority)\n- Protocol gateways enable monitoring at boundary (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: OT protocol cannot be secured", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Physical constraints preventing security equipment installation - is this waivable?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Physical infeasibility requires demonstration of all alternatives explored", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Compensating controls must provide equivalent protection", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "CSA may request site inspection to verify constraints", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Building cannot accommodate additional security equipment**\n\n**Sector Context:** Transportation CII\n\n**Decision:** Maybe, if alternatives explored and ineffective\n\n**Reasoning:**\nPhysical constraints may be waivable if genuinely infeasible. Must explore all alternatives first. Compensating controls may include: enhanced monitoring, procedural controls, security personnel presence. May require CSA site inspection.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Physical infeasibility requires demonstration of all alternatives explored (critical priority)\n- Compensating controls must provide equivalent protection (critical priority)\n- CSA may request site inspection to verify constraints (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Building cannot accommodate additional security equipment", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What happens when CSA denies a waiver application?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Waiver denial results in compliance directive with timeline", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Enforcement actions escalate for continued non-compliance", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Organization must implement control regardless of challenges when waiver denied", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Waiver denial and enforcement**\n\n**Sector Context:** Energy CII\n\n**Decision:** Denial triggers enforcement process\n\n**Reasoning:**\nWaiver denial triggers enforcement process. CSA may issue compliance directive with timeline for implementation. Organization must demonstrate progress. Continued non-compliance may lead to enforcement action including fines or direction.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Waiver denial results in compliance directive with timeline (critical priority)\n- Enforcement actions escalate for continued non-compliance (critical priority)\n- Organization must implement control regardless of challenges when waiver denied (critical priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Waiver denial and enforcement", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "1.4.7"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"], "clause_reference_original": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "PARTIAL_ACCEPT"}}
{"test_id": "B22-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What conditions might CSA attach to granting a waiver?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Conditional waivers have specific compliance requirements attached", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Periodic reporting demonstrates ongoing compliance efforts", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Sunset clause forces re-evaluation at specified date", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Conditional waiver with milestones**\n\n**Sector Context:** Healthcare CII\n\n**Decision:** CSA may impose conditions\n\n**Reasoning:**\nCSA may grant conditional waiver with: 1) strict compensating control requirements, 2) periodic progress reports, 3) sunset clause with review date, 4) mandatory transition planning, 5) additional monitoring requirements.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Conditional waivers have specific compliance requirements attached (critical priority)\n- Periodic reporting demonstrates ongoing compliance efforts (critical priority)\n- Sunset clause forces re-evaluation at specified date (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Conditional waiver with milestones", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "CII system needs immediate replacement for security reasons but budget not available until next fiscal year - can waiver bridge the gap?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Budget constraints are managed through risk acceptance, not waiver", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Interim controls must reduce risk during budget gap period", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "CSA notification required if interim controls don't meet standards", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Emergency equipment replacement**\n\n**Sector Context:** Banking CII\n\n**Decision:** Temporary risk acceptance, not waiver\n\n**Reasoning:**\nThis is not a waiver but temporary risk acceptance. Document interim controls, budget request for next year, contingency planning. May require CSA notification if risk is significant. Not a waiver from requirement.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Budget constraints are managed through risk acceptance, not waiver (critical priority)\n- Interim controls must reduce risk during budget gap period (critical priority)\n- CSA notification required if interim controls don't meet standards (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Emergency equipment replacement", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "How should a waiver application address conflicting consultant recommendations?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "All expert opinions should be disclosed with reasoning", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Evidence-based justification preferred over authority-based arguments", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "CSA evaluates technical merits of competing approaches", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Conflicting expert opinions**\n\n**Sector Context:** Government CII\n\n**Decision:** Evidence-based approach required\n\n**Reasoning:**\nPresent all expert opinions with evidence. Acknowledge differing recommendations. Explain chosen approach with technical justification. CSA will evaluate based on evidence presented. Transparency about expert conflicts strengthens application.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- All expert opinions should be disclosed with reasoning (critical priority)\n- Evidence-based justification preferred over authority-based arguments (critical priority)\n- CSA evaluates technical merits of competing approaches (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Conflicting expert opinions", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "What if vendor supply chain issues prevent implementing required controls within CSA timeline?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Timeline extension may be granted with documented justification", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Supply chain documentation must show mitigation efforts", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Partial waiver may cover extension period gap", "tier": "supporting", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Supply chain prevents implementation**\n\n**Sector Context:** Telecommunications CII\n\n**Decision:** Timeline extension may be requested\n\n**Reasoning:**\nRequest timeline extension from CSA citing supply chain constraints. Document: vendor communications, alternative approaches explored, interim controls implemented. May need partial waiver for extension period. Demonstrate good faith effort.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- Timeline extension may be granted with documented justification (critical priority)\n- Supply chain documentation must show mitigation efforts (critical priority)\n- Partial waiver may cover extension period gap (supporting priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Supply chain prevents implementation", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B22-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B22", "input": {"question": "Can limited overnight staff justify waiving the 2-hour critical incident notification requirement?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "2-hour critical notification is not waivable for staff constraints", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Alternative processes must ensure notification within timeline", "tier": "critical", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}, {"fact": "Third-party monitoring services can provide 24/7 coverage", "tier": "important", "source": "Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7) and CCoP 2.0 Section 1.6"}], "expected_response": "**Waiver Assessment: Waiver for 2-hour notification due to limited staff**\n\n**Sector Context:** Water CII\n\n**Decision:** No, alternative process required\n\n**Reasoning:**\n2-hour notification is mandatory for critical incidents. Limited staff is not grounds for waiver. Must establish alternative process: on-call rotation, third-party monitoring service, automated alerting with escalation to available personnel.\n\n**Key Considerations:**\n- 2-hour critical notification is not waivable for staff constraints (critical priority)\n- Alternative processes must ensure notification within timeline (critical priority)\n- Third-party monitoring services can provide 24/7 coverage (important priority)\n\n\n**Waiver Process Requirements (if applicable):**\n1. **Documentation:** Technical infeasibility evidence, compensating controls, transition plan\n2. **Justification:** Business case for why requirement cannot be met\n3. **Timeline:** Duration requested and renewal planning\n4. **Alternatives Considered:** Demonstrate exploration of all options\n5. **CSA Consultation:** Early engagement with CSA on complex waiver scenarios\n\n**Risk Acceptance (if waiver denied):**\nIf waiver is not appropriate, risk must be formally accepted with documented compensating controls and CSA notification where required.\n\nReference: Cybersecurity Act Section 11(7), CCoP 2.0 Section 11", "reasoning_chain": ["Analyze scenario: Waiver for 2-hour notification due to limited staff", "Determine if waiver is appropriate under Section 11(7)", "Identify compensating controls if waiver applicable"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating cost or convenience alone justifies waiver", "Suggesting CSA waiver process is optional when mandatory", "Confusing risk acceptance with waiver", "Clear waiver decision (yes/no/alternative)", "Justification for the decision", "Compensating control requirements if applicable"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific CSA waiver approval criteria", "Creating non-existent timelines or procedures"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "high", "scenario_type": "waiver_reasoning", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
